message: "If you use PrivScale-FL in your research, please cite:"
title: "Privacy Risk Scales with Effective Dimension in Federated Learning"
type: conference-paper
authors:
  - name: "Anonymous Authors"
    affiliation: "Anonymous Institution"
conference:
  name: "Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)"
year: 2026
repository-code: "https://anonymous.4open.science/r/[ID]/"
abstract: >
  Privacy mechanisms in federated learning are typically calibrated
  independently of model scale, implicitly assuming that privacy risk remains
  stable as models grow. We challenge this assumption by introducing federated
  leakage, a signal-level quantity measuring how much client-specific
  information survives in privatized local update trajectories, and by
  identifying effective model dimension as the key scaling variable. We derive
  a conditional scaling law showing that federated leakage grows as
  Theta(d / log d) with effective dimension d, show that fixed-noise DP-FL
  protocols become progressively miscalibrated, and propose a scale-aware
  noise calibration rule that preserves a target leakage regime.
keywords:
  - federated learning
  - differential privacy
  - privacy scaling
  - effective dimension
  - membership inference
  - model scaling
  - privacy debt
